/*******************************************************************************
                    What Is parser Doing
*******************************************************************************/
    parser turns a rule's TERMS into a generated parse body, compiles it, and
    installs it -- replacing the interpreted term-walk for that rule and every
    sub-rule reachable from it.

        parser(SomeRule);

    Two phases, in this order, and the order is the whole design:

        walkRules(argument)      GENERATE. Emit a body for the root and for
                                 every rule under it. Installs nothing.
        compileRules(argument)   INSTALL. setParse classifies and binds the
                                 parse method; compile parses the emitted text
                                 into a BlocK that can actually run.

    Nothing is installed during the walk. That split is deliberate -- a walk
    that installed as it went would hit rules mid-classification, and setParse
    leaves an already-installed rule alone, so a premature raise silently ends
    the job (fixIts F-90).

    WHAT generateParse EMITS. It walks the rule's attributes in order, skipping
    noPrint ones, and writes a call per term:

        NamE = CodE { return first() && nameSet(); }
        GrouP = CodE { return NamE() || QuotE(); }

    The connective is chosen by hasTraitS -- && when the rule has real terms,
    || when it does not. noPrint is this tree's standing "artifact, not a term"
    mark, which is why CodE, builtinActoR and builtinParseR never appear in an
    emitted body even though they are attributes like any other.

    A rule carrying DATA rather than a list is refused: it is appended to the
    uncompiled field and generation returns. A rule with both data and a list is
    a contradiction and says so on stderr.

    WHERE THE BODY GOES -- and this is the part that changed on 2026-09-20.
    The generated parse is parked in a builtinParseR carrier, which is a noPrint
    attribute holding its own CodE, exactly mirroring builtinActoR:

        SomeRule            length 6
            <its terms>
            builtinActoR        noPrint            the ACTION, if it has one
                CodE                               the action body
            builtinParseR       noPrint            the generated PARSE
                CodE                               the emitted text
                BlocK                              once compiled

    THE RULE ITSELF CARRIES NO CodE. That is what retired ownActionAtEntry: the
    old code had to ask "does this rule already have an action in CodE, so that
    writing the parse there would delete it" -- and once the action moved to its
    own carrier that question has no instances. Two carriers, each with exactly
    one meaning, and nothing to contest.

    WHY TWO CARRIERS AND NOT ONE SLOT WITH A RULE. A rule can carry an action AND
    a generated parse at the same time. A single "where is the body" answer would
    have to mean two things at once, which is this project's most expensive
    recurring shape. So GroupItem carries two accessor trios --
    actionHolder/actionBody/actionBlocK and parseHolder/parseBody/parseBlocK --
    and every reader names the one it means. processCode takes the holder as a
    second argument for the same reason.

    WHO FIRES WHAT, afterwards:
        parseRule          fires builtinParseR's BlocK -- the parse
        fireLabelMethod    fires builtinActoR's method -- the action
    Two seats over two bodies, sharing nothing.

/*******************************************************************************
                    parser Usage Guide
*******************************************************************************/
    THE CALL TAKES THE RULE, NOT ITS NAME.

        parser(Search);

    The root arrives as the rule through *argument, so registry membership stops
    mattering -- the old Grokking[argument.taG] spelling could only ever reach a
    rule living in Grokking.

    THIS FILE IS DEFINITIONS ONLY. It has no preamble and no driver, on purpose.
    An includer has already done Start(), the includes and the search stack, and
    its own preamble would be circular the moment utilities includes this file.
    A live driver at the foot would fire -- and stop() -- inside whoever included
    it. The driver lives in incant/pop/parserT.

    WHAT A GOOD RUN LOOKS LIKE, per rule:

        Generating parse code for Search
        Search = CodE { return search() && followedBy() && GrouP() && SemI(); }
        ...
        compile succeeded for Search
        end compileRules

    A "Generating parse code for X" with no "X = CodE {...}" line after it means
    X was refused -- data-bearing, or already carrying a parse.

    HOW TO INSPECT THE RESULT. Pass the rule as an argument; do not name it as a
    bare statement.

        dumpContents(Search);                      the rule, carrier visible
        dumpContents(Search["builtinParseR"]);     CodE, and BlocK once compiled

    ⚠ NEVER TRUTH-TEST A RULE-SHAPED VALUE. `if someRule;` is a CALL, not a read
    -- it fires the rule's action, and some actions clear their input. The test
    that asked "did this work" has destroyed what it was asking about. Assert on
    an artifact a fired action cannot fake: a subscript that finds a carrier, or
    a list length. This is bear-trap #34 and it has bitten the investigation as
    well as the code.

    ⚠⚠ DO NOT PUT STATEMENTS AFTER A DRIVE OF A GENERATED PARSE. Today,
